**Notation.** For each $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $u_n : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the "clipping" function
$$u_n(x) = \begin{cases} -n, & x \le -n, \\ x, & -n < x \le n, \\ n, & x > n. \end{cases}$$
Note $u_n$ is continuous (piecewise affine, agreeing at the junctions $x = \pm n$) and satisfies $|u_n(x)| \le n$ with $u_n(x) = x$ whenever $|x| \le n$.

We prove both directions.

---

**Direction ($\Rightarrow$): $F$ continuous $\Rightarrow$ $u_n \circ F$ continuous for all $n$.**

Since $u_n$ is continuous for every $n$ and the composition of continuous functions is continuous, $u_n \circ F$ is continuous for every $n$. ∎

---

**Direction ($\Leftarrow$): $u_n \circ F$ continuous for all $n$ $\Rightarrow$ $F$ continuous.**

We show $F$ is continuous at an arbitrary point $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}$.

Let $\varepsilon > 0$. Choose $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with
$$n > |F(x_0)| + \varepsilon.$$
Then $|F(x_0)| < n$, so $u_n(F(x_0)) = F(x_0)$.

Since $u_n \circ F$ is continuous at $x_0$, there exists $\delta > 0$ such that for all $x$ with $|x - x_0| < \delta$,
$$|u_n(F(x)) - u_n(F(x_0))| < \varepsilon. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Claim.** For $|x - x_0| < \delta$, we have $u_n(F(x)) = F(x)$, i.e., $|F(x)| \le n$ is not violated on the side where clipping occurs.

*Proof of claim.* Suppose for contradiction that $F(x) \ge n$ for some such $x$. Then $u_n(F(x)) = n$, so by ($\ast$),
$$|n - F(x_0)| < \varepsilon.$$
Since $n > |F(x_0)| \ge F(x_0)$, we have $n - F(x_0) > 0$, hence $|n - F(x_0)| = n - F(x_0) \ge n - |F(x_0)| > \varepsilon$, contradicting ($\ast$).

Similarly, suppose $F(x) \le -n$. Then $u_n(F(x)) = -n$, so by ($\ast$),
$$|{-n - F(x_0)}| = |F(x_0) + n| < \varepsilon.$$
Since $n > |F(x_0)|$, we have $F(x_0) + n \ge n - |F(x_0)| > \varepsilon > 0$, so $|F(x_0) + n| = F(x_0) + n > \varepsilon$, again contradicting ($\ast$).

Thus neither $F(x) \ge n$ nor $F(x) \le -n$ can occur, so $|F(x)| \le n$ and $u_n(F(x)) = F(x)$. ∎ (claim)

Using the claim in ($\ast$): for $|x - x_0| < \delta$,
$$|F(x) - F(x_0)| = |u_n(F(x)) - u_n(F(x_0))| < \varepsilon.$$

This proves $F$ is continuous at $x_0$. Since $x_0$ was arbitrary, $F$ is continuous on $\mathbb{R}$. ∎

---

Combining both directions, $F$ is continuous if and only if $u_n \circ F$ is continuous for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

